/*! \page developer_build_makefile_page Makefiles

\ref developer_build_pattern_page  (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_standards_page      (Next)
<BR>

Almost every subdirectory of the src directory tree contains a makefile
(except the src/tests directory). There are also makefiles in the root 
and namespace-level directories of the bld directory tree, though not
lower level subdirectories. Each of these makefiles contains a rule for 
a default target named "all" that constructs all of the targets for which 
that makefile has responsibility.

Many PSCF makefiles share a similar structure, but there are differences
in the contents of makefiles located at different levels of the PSCF 
directory tree. Different types of makefiles are found in the following 
locations:

   - **Low level subdirectories of src** : These are makefiles that are
     located within subdirectories of one of the namespace-level directory
     trees within the src directory. The "all" target of each such makefile
     creates a set of object target files by compiling all of the source
     files that are located in the tree rooted at the directory containing
     the makefile (i.e., in that directory and any enclosed subdirectories).

   - **Namespace-level (not program-level) subdirectories of src or bld**:
     The "all" target of makefiles in the util, pscf, and prdc
     namespace-level directories in both the src or bld directory each 
     creates a static library file, in addition to compiling all of the
     source files in that directory tree.

   - **Program-level subdirectories of src or bld (not program level)**:
     The "all" target of makefiles in the r1d and rp directories of both
     src or bld directory each creates one or more executable files, in
     addition to compiling a set of source files and building a static 
     library.

   - **src and bld directories** : The "all" target of a makefile located
     in the root of either the src or bld directory tree compiles the
     entire PSCF package by recursively invoking make in all namespace 
     level directories. 

   - **PSCF root directory** : The "all" target of the master makefile
     in the PSCF root directory recursively invokes make in the bld
     directory in order to performs an out-of-source build of the
     entire package.

Each of these types of makefile is discussed below, starting with makefiles 
in the lowest level of the src directory tree, and working our way up.

\section developer_build_makefile_srcsub_sec Makefiles in lower level subdirectories of src

The simplest PSCF makefiles are those that that are located in
subdirectories of one of the namespace-level directories within the
src directory tree. Analogous lower-level directories of the bld 
directory tree do not contain makefiles. As an example, the contents 
of the makefile in the directory src/rp/fields is shown below:

\verbatim
#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include *.mk makefile fragments

include ../../config.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/patterns.mk

#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main targets

all: $(rp_field_OBJS)

clean:
	rm -f $(rp_field_OBJS) $(rp_field_OBJS:.o=.d)

#--------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include dependency files

-include $(rp_field_OBJS:.o=.d)

\endverbatim

This makefile has three main sections that are separated by horizontal
lines and labelled by comments. Makefile lines that start with a \#
character are comment lines thatare ignored by the make command parser.

<b> Including *.mk makefile fragments: </b>:

The first section of this file contains three "include" directives that
include makefile fragments that are needed in the rest of the file.

The directive "../../config.mk" includes the src/config.mk configuration
file in the src directory.  The path for this file is given here using
a relative path in which the symbol ".." refers to a parent directory.
The path "../.." thus refers to the parent of the parent of the current
working directory. In this example, the make command would use this
makefile only if were invoked from the directory \$(ROOT_DIR)/src/rp/field
that contains this makefile.  The path "../.." is thus the relative
path to the PSCF src directory from the current directory in which
make was invoked.

A relative path must be used to include the relevant config.mk file
in every PSCF makefile because the makefile variable SRC_DIR and 
BLD_DIR that provides absolute paths to the src directory and the 
build directory are defined in config.mk file. These variables thus 
cannot be used to define paths until after this file is included. 
Note that later include directives for the required sources.mk and 
patterns.mk files can and do use values of these variables to 
specify the locations of these files.

The directive "#include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/sources.mk" includes the
sources.mk file in the src/rp/field directory. This file contains 
the definition of the variable src_field_OBJS that is used in the 
rest of this makefile. This variable is assigned a value in the file
src/rp/field/sources.mk, which is then included by src/rp/sources.mk

The directive "#include $(SRC_DIR)/rp/patterns.mk" includes the
src/rp/patterns.mk file that contains the relevant pattern rule for
compiling *.cpp and *.cu files with the src/rp directory tree.

<b> The "all" target: </b>:

The makefile rule for the "all" target is given by the line
\verbatim
all: $(rp_field_OBJS)
\endverbatim
The variable value \$(rp_field_OBJS) expands to a list of absolute
paths to all of the object file targets associated with source files
in the subdirectory src/rp/field that contains this makefile.

By listing all of these object file targets as pre-requisites, the
rule for the "all" target forces make to check on the status of all
of these object files, and update any of them that do not exist or
are out-of-date.  A pattern rule for defining *.cpp sources files
that is defined in the file src/rp/patterns.mk is used to create
or recreate each required object file pre-requisite, as needed.

<b> Including dependency files </b>:

In order to check whether a particular object file is out-of-date, make
needs access to the dependency file associated with that object file.
The last part of this example file includes the dependency files 
associated with all of the object file targets in the src/rp/field 
directory. This is accomplished by the directive
\verbatim
-include $(rp_field_DEPS)
\endverbatim
This directive includes all existing *.d dependency files that are 
associated with object files listed in the variable $(rp_field_OBJS).
The dash ("-") that appears before the "include" keyword is a special 
symbol that instructs the make command to quietly ignore failures that 
may occur if one or more of the more dependency files in this list do 
not exist.

Each dependency files is created as a side effect of a pattern rule that
also creates an object file target. As a result, the dependency file
associated with an object file does not exist before the first time that
the object file is created.  When a file that is the target of a rule
does not exist, however, the target is always considered out-of-date.
As a result, an object file target that does yet exist will thus always be 
built as needed, either if the rule to construct the target is invoked,
or if that file is listed as a pre-requisite for some other target. The
list of prerequisistes of an object file target is thus only needed after
the file is first created, to determine whether an <em>existing</em>
object file is or is not out-of-date.

\section developer_build_makefile_namespace_sec Makefiles in namespace-level directories (not program-level)

As already discussed, the src/ and bld/ directories have analogous
internal structures, and either can be used as the "build" directory
for, respectively, out-of-source or in-source builds.  The src and bld
directories each contain 5 subdirectories named named util/, pscf/,
prdc/ r1d/, and rp/ that we refer to as namespace-level directories.
Each namespace-level subdirectories in the bld/ directory contains a
makefiles that is a copy of the makefile located in the corresponding 
subdirectories of the src/ directory. These makefiles in the bld/
directory are created by the configure script by copying corresponding
makefiles located in the src/ directory.

The namespace-level directories named r1d/ and rp/ are distinguished by 
the fact that each of these directories contains at one or more source 
files that each define the main function for an executable program.
We refer to these two directories as program-level directories. 
Makefiles in program-level directories contain additional rules to 
compile any main program files and create one or more executables.  
The remainder of this section discusses the makefiles in the three 
namespace-level directories named util/, pscf/ and prdc/ that are 
**not** also program-level directories.

The main difference between the makefiles in the util/, pscf/ and prdc/
namepace-level directories and those found in lower level subdirectories 
of the src directory is that the makefile in each namespace-level directory 
builds a static library that contains the contents of all of the object
files in the same directory. The rule to create the static library file 
is defined in the sources.mk file in each of these namespace-level 
directories.

As an example, the body of the makefile in the src/prdc directory is
shown below. The makefile variable value \$(prdc_LIB) is defined in 
the included file \$(SRC_DIR)/prdc/sources.mk file, and gives the path 
\$(BLD_DIR)/prdc/libprdc.a to the static library file target in the 
prdc namespace-level directory of the build directory.

\verbatim
#------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include *.mk makefile fragments

include ../config.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/prdc/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/prdc/patterns.mk

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main targets

all: $(prdc_LIB)

clean:
	rm -f $(prdc_OBJS) $(prdc_OBJS:.o=.d)
	rm -f $(prdc_LIB)
	rm -f *.o */*.o */*/*.o
	rm -f *.d */*.d */*/*.d
	cd tests; $(MAKE) clean

very-clean:
	$(MAKE) clean

.PHONY: all clean veryclean

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include dependency files

-include $(prdc_OBJS:.o=.d)
\endverbatim

The main difference between the structure of this file and that of the
makefiles in lower-level subdirectories of src is that, in this example,
the rule for the "all" target contains the path $(PRDC_LIB) to a static
library file target as its only pre-requisite. The rule to build the
library $(PRDC_LIB), which is defined in the file src/prdc/sources.mk,
gives the associated object file list \$(prdc_OBJS) as a prerequisite.
As a result, any attempt to build or update the library file will 
force creation or recreation of any out-of-date object files in the 
list \$(prdc_OBJS), as needed.  

The use of a relative path ../config.mk in the directive that includes
the configuration file allows make to include the bld/config.mk when
make is invoked from a namespace-level subdirectory of the bld directory
but to include src/config.mk when make is invoked from a namespace-level
subdirectory of the src directory. 

\section developer_build_makefile_program_sec Makefiles in program-level directories

We now consider makefiles located in the r1d and rp program-level
subdirectories of the src and bld directories. These makefiles each define
two additional types of rules that do not exist in makefiles located in 
the other namespace-level directories. The first additional type of rule 
gives instructions to compile a main program file to create an associated
object file.  The second additional type of rule gives instructions for
how to link the resulting object file to several libraries in order to
create an executable file.  Because the executable file is created by a 
rule that links to library files that are located in other namespace-level 
directories, the makefile in each program-level directories must include 
source list (sources.mk) and dependency files (*.d) files from other
namespace-level directories.

As an example, here is the body of the makefile in r1d program-level
directory:
\verbatim
#------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include *.mk makefile fragments

include ../config.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/util/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/pscf/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/r1d/sources.mk
include $(SRC_DIR)/r1d/patterns.mk

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Paths to pscf_r1d program files

# Base path for pscf_r1d.o and pscf_r1d.d intermediate files 
PSCF_R1D=$(BLD_DIR)/r1d/pscf_r1d

# Path to pscf_r1d executable file
PSCF_R1D_EXE=$(BIN_DIR)/pscf_r1d

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Main targets 

all: $(r1d_OBJS) $(r1d_LIB) $(PSCF_R1D_EXE)

clean:
	rm -f $(r1d_OBJS)
	rm -f $(r1d_DEPS) 
	rm -f $(r1d_LIB)
	rm -f $(PSCF_R1D).o $(PSCF_R1D).d
	rm -f *.o */*.o
	rm -f *.d */*.d
	cd tests; $(MAKE) clean

# Executable pscf_r1d file (linking)
$(PSCF_R1D_EXE): $(PSCF_R1D).o $(PSCF_LIBS)
	$(CXX) $(LDFLAGS) -o $(PSCF_R1D_EXE) $(PSCF_R1D).o $(LIBS) 

.PHONY: all clean pscf_r1d

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------
# Include dependency files

-include $(util_DEPS)
-include $(pscf_DEPS)
-include $(r1d_DEPS)
-include $(PSCF_R1D).d
\endverbatim

Note the following elements of this file that are not present in the
makefiles in the util, pscf, and prdc namespace-level directories:

  - Definition of a variable PSCF_R1D = \$(BLD_DIR)/r1d/pscf_r1d that 
    gives the common part of the paths for the object and dependency 
    files that are created by compiling the program source file 
    src/r1d/pscf_r1d.cpp, without the .o and .d file name suffixes.

  - Definition of a variable PSCF_PC_EXE = \$(BIN_DIR)/pscf_r1d that 
    gives the absolute path to the resulting executable file, which 
    is installed in the in bin directory by default. 

  - A rule to build the executable file \$(PSCF_PC_EXE) by linking the main
    program object file \$(PSCF_PC).o to all relevant library files.

  - The "all" target lists the main program executable \$(PSCF_PC_EXE) as
    a prerequisite.

  - Include directives to source.mk files and dependency files that are
    located in the util and pscf namespace-level directories, as well as 
    those located in the r1d directory that contains this makefile.

  - An include directive to include the dependency file \$(PSCF_PC).d
    associated with the main program source file.

Inclusion of sources.mk and dependency files from other namespace level
directories is necessary in a makefile in a program-level namespace to 
allow the make command to check whether the libraries located in other 
directories are up to date before linking to them to create an 
executable, and to allow the system to build or rebuild any object and 
library files that are found to be out-of-date.  The rules to build 
the static library in each relevant namespace-level directory is 
defined in the sources.mk file in that directory, which is included by 
this makefile.

Inclusion of source list and dependency files from other namespace-level
directories is **not** necessary in the makefiles located in the util,
pscf, and prdc directories because these makefiles do not define a rule
that links static libraries from different namespace-level directories
to create an executable.

In this example, the object file \$(PSCF_PC).o is created by compiling
the main program file h$(SRC_DIR)/rpc/pscf_rpc.cpp. This object file will
be created before the executable file because the object file is listed
as a prerequisite of the executable file target. 

The main program object file is not included in the list of files that
are archived to create an associated static library. For example, in the
rpc namespace-level directory, the file \$(PSCF_PC).o is not included in
the list \$(rpc_OBJS) of object files that are collected into the library
file \$(BLD_DIR)/rpc/librpc.a .

Explicit inclusion \$(rpc_OBJS) and \$(rpc_LIB) as prerequisites of the
"all" target is redundant, but these are included to help document the
sequence of actions triggered by the "all" target.  Any required building
or re-building of these object and library file targets would in any case
be triggered by a request to build the executable target \$(PSCF_PC_EXE),
because the rule for \$(PSCF_PC_EXE) lists \$(rpc_LIB) as a pre-requisite,
and the rule for \$(prc_LIB) lists the files in the list \$(prc_OBJS) as
prerequisites. A more concise version of this rule could thus list the
the executable file target as the only required prerequisite of "all".

\section developer_build_makefile_upper_sec Makefiles in src and bld directories

The makefiles in src and bld directories, which are identical, each contain
an "all" target that operates by recursively invoking make from within
namespace-level directories. Below, we show the essential parts of these
makefiles that are necessary to see how the "all" target is implemented:
\verbatim
include config.mk

# ======================================================================
# Main build targets

# Build all CPU and GPU programs
all:
	$(MAKE) all-cpu
ifdef PSCF_CUDA
	cd rpg; $(MAKE) all
endif

# Build all programs that uses exclusively CPU hardware
all-cpu:
	cd util; $(MAKE) all
	cd pscf; $(MAKE) all
	cd r1d; $(MAKE) all
	cd prdc; $(MAKE) all
	cd rpc; $(MAKE) all

   .
   .
   .

# ======================================================================
\endverbatim

Here \$(MAKE) is the value of a standard variable that represents the
name of the unix make command. The two target shown here both contain
recipes that instruct the system to repeatedly descend into a namespace
level directories. The "all-cpu" target is designed to build only the
two programs that use standard CPU hardware, pscf_r1d and pscf_rpc. The
"all" target is designed to also create the pscf_rpg executable if
compilation of CUDA code has been enabled by defining the PSCF_CUDA
makefile variable.

The phony "all-cpu" target simply invokes make all within 5 of the
6 namespace-level subdirectories, including the two program-level
subdirectories r1d and rpc that build the CPU programs pscf_r1d and
pscf_rpc, but excluding subdirectory rpg that contains CUDA C++ code
for pscf_rpg.

The "all" target first invokes cpu-all and then conditionally invokes
"make all" from within the rpg subdirectory if and only if the makefile
variable PSCF_CUDA is defined. The variable PSCF_CUDA is undefined by
default, and is defined if and only if the user has explicitly enabled
compilation of CUDA code. The "all" target thus compiles code in the rpg
directory and creates the pscf_rpg executable only if CUDA compilation
has been enabled.  Compilation of CUDA code can be enabled either by
using the setopts script, using the -c option with argument 1, or by
manually uncommenting the definition of the variable PSCF_CUDA in the
relevant config.mk configuration file.

The "all" target is the first target in the identical makefiles located
in the src and bld directory, and is the default target. Invoking "make"
with no target from either of these directories is thus equivalent
to invoking "make all" from the same location.

The remainder of this makefile defines a number of other targets that
enable the user to compile only part of the package, to run unit tests,
and to clean up after compilation. The purposes of these other targets
are explained by comments in the makefile, and are also generally
rather self-explanatory.

\section developer_build_makefile_root_sec Makefile in the PSCF root directory

The rule for the "all" target in the makefile located in the PSCF root
directory is given by:
\verbatim
all:
	cd bld ; $(MAKE)
\endverbatim
Invoking this rule from the root directory is thus equivalent to invoking
"make all" from within the bld directory. The effect in either case is to
perform an out-of-source build of the entire package.

A variety of other targets are defined in the makefile in the root
directory, but not discussed here. Purposes of these other targets are
explained by comments in this makefile. The recipes for most targets
in this makefile are simply wrappers that recursively invoke make from
within the bld, bld and src or docs directory, like the recipe for the
"all" target. Rather than repeating brief descriptions that are given
in the makefile, we recommend that users take a look at this makefile.

